    <body>
      <sec id="article-30182.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Tibial diaphyseal fractures are common long bone injuries that often result from high-energy trauma like motor vehicle accidents.&#x000a0;These injuries can also occur from low-energy falls in patients with weakened bone density, such as those with osteoporosis. The subcutaneous position of the tibia makes it vulnerable to open fractures and soft tissue compromise, which significantly increase infection risk and complicate management. Disruption of the nutrient artery and periosteal blood supply may impair bone healing and contribute to delayed union or nonunion. Patients typically present with localized pain, deformity, swelling, and inability to bear weight. Careful assessment for compartment syndrome and neurovascular injury is essential.</p>
        <p>Diagnosis relies on radiographic evaluation, with&#x000a0;computed tomography used selectively to define complex fracture patterns. Management includes nonoperative immobilization for stable fractures and operative fixation for displaced or unstable injuries, most commonly through intramedullary nailing using either a suprapatellar or infrapatellar approach. Complications include malunion, infection, and chronic pain. With timely, evidence-based, and interprofessional management, most patients achieve satisfactory functional recovery.</p>
        <p>This activity for healthcare professionals is designed to&#x000a0;improve learners' competence in evaluating and managing tibial diaphyseal fractures. Participants will advance their mastery of the condition's etiology, risk factors, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, and evidence-based diagnostic and therapeutic approaches.&#x000a0;Greater skills will equip clinicians to collaborate with interprofessional teams providing care for affected individuals.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify the clinical and diagnostic features of tibial diaphyseal fractures, ensuring&#x000a0;comprehensive neurovascular evaluation.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Select optimal imaging approaches for evaluating tibial diaphyseal fractures, incorporating&#x000a0;computed tomography&#x000a0;as indicated for suspected intra-articular extension.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Implement evidence-based, individualized approaches for managing&#x000a0;tibial diaphyseal fractures and avoiding&#x000a0;potential complications.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Collaborate with all members of the interprofessional team, including specialists such as radiologists, orthopedic surgeons, anesthesiologists, and&#x000a0;emergency physicians, to provide efficient, comprehensive, and coordinated care for patients with&#x000a0;tibial diaphyseal fractures.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=30182&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=30182">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30182.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Tibial shaft or diaphyseal fractures account for a substantial proportion of long-bone injuries in adults, with an incidence of approximately 16.9 per 100,000 persons per year, peaking among male individuals aged 10 to 20 years.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30182.r1">[1]</xref> These fractures frequently result from high-energy mechanisms and are particularly susceptible to open injury due to the tibia&#x02019;s subcutaneous anteromedial border.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30182.r2">[2]</xref> The lower leg is organized into&#x000a0;4 distinct fascial compartments, an anatomical configuration that predisposes patients to acute compartment syndrome, especially following high-energy or crush trauma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30182.r3">[3]</xref> Timely recognition and decompression via fasciotomy are essential to prevent irreversible neurovascular compromise and soft tissue necrosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30182.r4">[4]</xref></p>
        <p>Initial evaluation requires standard anteroposterior and lateral radiographs to confirm the diagnosis and assess alignment, fracture morphology, and joint involvement. Nonoperative management is appropriate for minimally displaced or stable fractures, whereas displaced, unstable, or open fractures generally require operative fixation to restore alignment, facilitate union, and reduce complications. Intramedullary nailing (IMN) represents the gold standard for most tibial shaft fractures due to its biomechanical stability and minimally invasive technique.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30182.r5">[5]</xref> Alternative interventions, including open reduction and internal fixation (ORIF) or external fixation, are indicated in cases involving complex fracture patterns, soft tissue compromise, or periarticular extension.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30182.r6">[6]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30182.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>Fractures of the tibial shaft frequently result from falls, motor vehicle collisions (MVCs), indoor incidents such as slipping or tripping on wet surfaces, and outdoor events, including running, cycling,&#x000a0;and contact sports. Injuries sustained during MVCs and sports occur more commonly in male individuals.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30182.r7">[7]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30182.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>Tibial shaft fractures are among the most common long-bone injuries in adults, accounting for approximately 2% of all fractures sustained annually. The overall incidence ranges from 16.9 to 21.5 per 100,000 persons per year, with a pronounced sex disparity. Men experience a higher incidence, at 21.5 per 100,000 per year, compared to 12.3 per 100,000 per year in women, a difference largely attributed to variations in injury mechanisms and exposure to high-risk events. Peak incidence in male patients occurs between 10 and 20 years of age, reaching 43.5 per 100,000 per year, predominantly due to high-energy trauma such as sports injuries and MVCs.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30182.r8">[8]</xref> In female patients, the highest incidence is observed between 20 and 30 years, reflecting distinct biomechanical and social risk factors.</p>
        <p>Tibial shaft fractures most frequently result from high-energy trauma, including MVCs, falls from height, and sports-related injuries. In older adults, particularly women, low-energy trauma such as simple falls may produce fractures in osteoporotic bone. Recognition of age- and sex-specific epidemiologic patterns is critical for developing targeted prevention strategies and optimizing trauma system resource allocation.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30182.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>The tibia is a long bone with a triangular cross-sectional geometry and functions as the primary weight-bearing structure of the lower extremity, transmitting approximately 80% to 85% of axial load during ambulation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30182.r9">[9]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30182.r10">[10]</xref> In proximal 3rd tibial shaft fractures, malalignment is frequently determined by characteristic deforming muscular forces acting on the fracture fragments.</p>
        <p>The patellar tendon and extensor mechanism exert an anteriorly directed force on the proximal fragment, maintaining relative extension, while the gastrocnemius muscle pulls the distal fragment posteriorly, producing a flexion deformity. Medial insertion of the pes anserinus complex can induce varus angulation of the proximal fragment. These deforming vectors underscore the necessity of meticulous reduction and stabilization in proximal tibial fractures to minimize residual malalignment and optimize functional outcomes.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30182.s6" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>Detailed history-taking and a careful physical examination are critical in the early assessment of suspected tibial shaft fractures. Determination of the injury mechanism provides critical insight into trauma energy and the likelihood of associated injuries. Low-energy mechanisms, such as ground-level falls, are more common in adults who are of advanced age&#x000a0;or have osteoporosis, whereas high-energy trauma, including MVCs, falls from height,&#x000a0;and crush injuries, predominates in younger individuals and often indicates complex fracture patterns.</p>
        <p>In fall-related trauma, distinguishing between mechanical falls and syncopal events is necessary, as the latter may require further cardiac or neurological assessment. In high-energy trauma, adherence to a systematic primary and secondary survey following Advanced Trauma Life Support (ATLS) principles is imperative to identify life-threatening injuries and prioritize management. Evaluation of the affected limb within the context of polytrauma should include careful assessment for ipsilateral injuries to the femur, knee, or ankle, as such injuries may influence both diagnostic considerations and treatment strategies.</p>
        <p>Local examination of the injured extremity should commence with a systematic skin inspection to identify open fractures, indicated by wounds, punctures, or lacerations near the fracture site. Open fractures require immediate classification, such as with the Gustilo-Anderson system, and urgent management to minimize infection risk. Swelling, deformity, and ecchymosis should be carefully documented.</p>
        <p>A complete neurovascular assessment of the affected limb is mandatory. Sensory and motor function of the peroneal and tibial nerves should be evaluated and compared with the contralateral side. Vascular assessment includes palpation of the dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial pulses, supplemented by Doppler ultrasonography if pulses are weak or absent. Additional perfusion indicators, including capillary refill, skin temperature, and color, should be assessed. Serial neurovascular examinations are essential, particularly when concern for developing compartment syndrome arises.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30182.s7" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>
<bold>Plain Radiography</bold>
</p>
        <p>Plain radiography is the initial imaging&#x000a0;technique&#x000a0;of choice for suspected tibial shaft fractures (see <bold>Image</bold>.&#x000a0;Tibial Diaphyseal&#x000a0;Fracture on Radiography). Standard anteroposterior and lateral views of the entire tibia are recommended, including the knee and ankle joints. Inclusion of adjacent joints facilitates detection of associated injuries and allows assessment for fracture extension or dislocation at either articulation.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Computed Tomography</bold>
</p>
        <p>Computed tomography (CT) is not routinely indicated for isolated tibial shaft fractures but is essential when intra-articular extension is suspected, particularly involving the tibial plateau or plafond. CT imaging provides superior visualization of articular congruity and supports preoperative planning in complex fracture patterns or periarticular involvement.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Preoperative Evaluation</bold>
</p>
        <p>Preparation for surgical management requires a standard medical preoperative workup, including complete blood count, basic metabolic panel, and coagulation profile (prothrombin time and international normalized ratio) as appropriate. A chest radiograph and electrocardiogram are typically obtained, particularly in individuals&#x000a0;who are older than&#x000a0;40 years or&#x000a0;have known comorbidities. A preoperative cardiology consultation may be indicated in older adults or individuals with known or suspected cardiac disease to optimize perioperative risk stratification and planning.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30182.s8" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>
<bold>Nonoperative Management</bold>
</p>
        <p>Nonoperative management is an appropriate option for select tibial shaft fractures, particularly when the fracture exhibits or can be reduced to acceptable alignment parameters.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30182.r11">[11]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30182.r12">[12]</xref> This approach is generally reserved for closed, isolated, minimally displaced fractures in patients with intact soft tissue envelopes and no neurovascular compromise. Acceptable alignment criteria, as supported by multiple studies, include the following thresholds:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Less than 5&#x000b0; of coronal plane angulation,&#x000a0;denoting varus or valgus deformity</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Less than 10&#x000b0; of sagittal plane angulation,&#x000a0;indicating procurvatum or recurvatum deformity</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Less than 1 cm of limb shortening</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>5&#x000b0; to 10&#x000b0; of rotational malalignment</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Greater than 50% cortical contact between fracture fragments&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30182.r13">[13]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30182.r14">[14]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30182.r15">[15]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>In moderately displaced fractures, closed reduction may be performed to achieve these alignment goals prior to initiating conservative management. Once alignment is confirmed, long-leg casting constitutes the primary method of immobilization. Weight-bearing is initially restricted, followed by a gradual progression to full weight-bearing over several weeks as callus formation advances. Serial radiographs are essential to monitor for loss of reduction or delayed union, particularly during the first 6 to 8 weeks.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30182.r16">[16]</xref></p>
        <p>Although surgical fixation is more commonly indicated, particularly in younger and more active patients, evidence demonstrates that nonoperative management can achieve satisfactory outcomes in carefully selected cases, with union rates comparable to surgical treatment. Nonoperative care may be associated with longer time to union and a higher risk of malunion in some series, highlighting the importance of patient selection and rigorous monitoring.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Operative Management</bold>
</p>
        <p>The gold standard for surgical management of tibial shaft fractures is IMN due to its favorable biomechanical stability, minimally invasive nature, and high union rates. IMN&#x000a0;may be performed using&#x000a0;2 primary&#x000a0;approaches: infrapatellar and suprapatellar.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30182.r17">[17]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30182.r18">[18]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30182.r19">[19]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30182.r20">[20]</xref>&#x000a0;Traditionally, the infrapatellar technique has been the standard, employing either a transpatellar tendon incision or a medial or lateral paratendinous approach. Despite generally excellent outcomes, the infrapatellar approach is associated with well-documented complications, including anterior knee pain in up to 47% of patients, malalignment in 58% to 85%, and malunion in approximately 30% of cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30182.r21">[21]</xref></p>
        <p>The suprapatellar approach has gained widespread acceptance, particularly for fractures of the proximal 3rd of the tibial shaft, due to advantages in alignment control, reduced manipulation of the fracture site, easier reduction with lower intraoperative fluoroscopy time, and decreased rates of malunion and anterior knee pain.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30182.r22">[22]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30182.r23">[23]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30182.r24">[24]</xref> Clinical and biomechanical studies indicate that suprapatellar nailing is associated with improved operative ergonomics and reduced malalignment.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30182.r25">[25]</xref></p>
        <p>Despite these benefits, concerns remain regarding femoral damage and an increased risk of knee sepsis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30182.r26">[26]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30182.r27">[27]</xref> Arthroscopic and cadaveric investigations have also reported a notable incidence of iatrogenic chondral injury and intra-articular metallic debris.&#x000a0;However, current evidence suggests that these complications do not significantly affect functional outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30182.r28">[28]</xref> Refinement of surgical technique, including accurate portal placement and the use of protective sleeves, is essential to minimize the risk of intra-articular complications.</p>
        <p>ORIF using plates and screws comprises a valuable alternative in select tibial shaft fractures, particularly when&#x000a0;damage extends into the articular surface or intramedullary fixation is not feasible. Circular external fixation, such as the Taylor Spatial Frame, is indicated for complex fractures with severe soft tissue compromise or bone loss. The Taylor Spatial Frame permits gradual deformity correction and limb length restoration and may serve as either temporary stabilization or definitive fixation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30182.r29">[29]</xref></p>
        <p>Timely and meticulous debridement is critical in the management of open tibial fractures. However, recent evidence challenges the traditional view that intervention within&#x000a0;6 hours substantially alters outcomes, highlighting the importance of debridement quality and soft tissue management. Fasciotomy should be performed promptly when clinical suspicion of acute compartment syndrome exists, as delays may lead to irreversible neurovascular compromise and suboptimal functional recovery.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30182.s9" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>Lower extremity pain and swelling following trauma or prolonged immobilization may arise from conditions other than tibial shaft fracture, requiring careful consideration of the differential diagnosis. Localized hematoma can result from direct trauma to the lower leg, producing swelling in the absence of an underlying fracture. Compartment syndrome, characterized by extreme pain often disproportionate to the apparent injury, may develop not only in association with fracture but also in the setting of repetitive overuse or prolonged ground immobilization. Accurate differentiation among these conditions is essential, as timely recognition of compartment syndrome, in particular, is critical to prevent irreversible neurovascular compromise and optimize functional outcomes.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30182.s10" sec-type="Staging">
        <title>Staging</title>
        <p>
<bold>Tibial Fracture Classification</bold>
</p>
        <p>Fractures of the tibial shaft&#x000a0;may be categorized&#x000a0;formally using the&#x000a0;Arbeitsgemeinschaft f&#x000fc;r Osteosynthesefragen/Orthopaedic Trauma Association (AO/OTA) system, which is widely employed in clinical research and operative planning.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30182.r30">[30]</xref> The tibial shaft is designated as segment 42, and fractures are classified as follows:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Type A: Simple fractures</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Type B: Wedge fractures</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Type C: Complex (comminuted) fractures</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Further subclassification considers fracture location (proximal, middle, or distal 3rd) and the presence or absence of a concomitant fibular fracture. In addition to formal classification, tibial fractures may be described using a descriptive system, which incorporates the following parameters:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Fracture type: Closed or open, with open fractures graded according to the Gustilo-Anderson classification</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Location: Proximal 3rd, midshaft, or distal 3rd</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Morphology: Transverse, oblique, spiral, or comminuted</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>These descriptive terms are commonly applied in clinical practice to guide treatment decisions, predict outcomes, and facilitate communication among healthcare providers.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30182.s11" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>Longitudinal cohort studies following tibial shaft fractures indicate an initial decline in functional performance, with gradual recovery over 6 to 12 months.&#x000a0;However, many patients do not regain baseline functional scores at&#x000a0;5 years postoperatively.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30182.r31">[31]</xref> In long-term follow-up extending up to 12 years after IMN, approximately 73% of patients report persistent knee pain, and around 33% describe subjective leg swelling, highlighting the chronic nature of these sequelae.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30182.r32">[32]</xref>&#x000a0;Concomitant lower extremity injuries, advanced age, elevated body mass index, high-energy mechanisms of injury, and nonunion have been recognized as significant predictors of long-term reductions in ambulatory capacity following tibial diaphyseal fractures.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30182.r33">[33]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30182.s12" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>A thorough understanding of the common complications of tibial shaft fractures is essential for timely recognition and appropriate management to optimize patient outcomes.&#x000a0;Compartment syndrome is a critical early complication, with the first and most sensitive indicator being pain that is out of proportion to the injury or occurs with passive stretch.&#x000a0;Classical signs include palpable swelling, pallor, pulselessness, and paresthesias, although many of these manifestations develop later.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30182.r34">[34]</xref> In pediatric patients, compartment syndrome often presents as anxiety, agitation, and an increasing need for analgesics.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30182.r35">[35]</xref></p>
        <p>Malunion is another frequent complication, particularly in proximal tibial fractures, which tend to develop valgus angulation and apex anterior (procurvatum) deformity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30182.r36">[36]</xref> Nonunion, typically defined as the failure of a fracture to achieve radiographic healing by&#x000a0;6 months or the inability to heal without surgical intervention, also occurs in a subset of patients. Finally, anterior knee pain represents the most common long-term complication following&#x000a0;IMN insertion, affecting patient comfort and functional recovery.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30182.r37">[37]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30182.s13" sec-type="Postoperative and Rehabilitation Care">
        <title>Postoperative and Rehabilitation Care</title>
        <p>Assessment of recommended weight-bearing status is essential following tibial shaft fractures. Patients who have been treated nonoperatively, with external fixation, or&#x000a0;sustained comminuted fracture patterns frequently require weight-bearing restrictions, whereas simple fractures stabilized with intramedullary fixation may allow weight bearing as tolerated. Coordination with physical and occupational therapy is important to optimize functional recovery.</p>
        <p>Patients treated nonoperatively in a cast require close outpatient monitoring to detect loss of reduction. Cast modification may be necessary if reduction loss occurs during follow-up, or the fracture may require closed reduction under anesthesia to restore alignment.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30182.s14" sec-type="Consultations">
        <title>Consultations</title>
        <p>In high-energy trauma, including MVCs and falls from height, evaluation by the trauma team should include assessment for additional injuries. Extensive soft tissue loss or damage may necessitate intervention by plastic surgery for skin grafting or flap coverage. Treatment of patients with tibial shaft fractures often requires preoperative and postoperative optimization of comorbidities. Interprofessional care may involve specialists in orthopedics, geriatrics, internal medicine, trauma surgery, anesthesia, pharmacy, and other relevant subspecialties based on the patient&#x02019;s medical profile.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30182.s15" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>Measures to reduce the risk of tibial shaft fractures focus on minimizing exposure to high-energy trauma. Strategies include the use of protective equipment during sports, adherence to traffic safety regulations, and fall-prevention interventions in the home, particularly for older adults. Optimizing bone health through adequate nutrition, calcium and vitamin D intake, and regular weight-bearing exercise further decreases fracture susceptibility.</p>
        <p>Once a tibial fracture has occurred, early and appropriate management is critical to prevent adverse outcomes. Vital measures include timely reduction and stabilization, careful monitoring for neurovascular compromise, and adherence to recommended weight-bearing restrictions. Coordinated rehabilitation with physical and occupational therapy promotes functional recovery and reduces the risk of malunion, nonunion, and secondary injury.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30182.s16" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Most&#x000a0;patients with tibial shaft fractures present to the emergency department. Prompt acquisition of a thorough history, comprehensive physical examination, and appropriate imaging is essential. From a triage perspective, patients with open fractures or suspected compartment syndrome require urgent evaluation.</p>
        <p>When bone is visibly exposed or an open fracture is known, antibiotic therapy should be initiated immediately to reduce the risk of infection. Bedside debridement of gross contamination, along with provisional splinting, is also indicated. Antibiotic&#x000a0;administration should be continued into the postoperative period as appropriate.</p>
        <p>Recognition of the signs and symptoms of the limb-threatening condition, compartment syndrome, is essential for all members of the healthcare team. Compartment syndrome may develop in both open and closed tibial fractures and can occur before or after surgical intervention. The circumferential nature of casts may exacerbate or precipitate compartment syndrome, even in minimally displaced fractures. Patients presenting with mild swelling may deteriorate overnight.&#x000a0;Therefore, continuous monitoring of pain progression, particularly by floor nurses, is critical. Standard supportive measures, including application of ice and elevation of the affected extremity, should be employed for all patients with tibial shaft fractures.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30182.s17">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=30182&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=30182">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
          </list-item>
